<!--
  ============================================================================
  API SCOPE  |  content-safety-internal   (path: /internal/content-safety)
  ============================================================================
  WHY THIS API EXISTS — and it is not a workaround, it is the pattern
  ====================================================================
  `llm-content-safety` calls an APIM backend, and an APIM backend authenticates
  with a static credential. Providence's Foundry account has local auth
  disabled — verified on this account:

      az cognitiveservices account keys list ...
      -> BadRequest: Failed to list key. disableLocalAuth is set to be true

  That is the correct posture and it should not be relaxed to make a policy
  work. There is no key to put in the backend, so the backend cannot call
  Content Safety directly.

  The mediation pattern: expose Content Safety as an internal APIM API whose
  policy attaches the gateway's own managed identity, then point the
  `content-safety` backend at that API. The keyless boundary moves from the
  backend definition to a policy, which is where it can be expressed.

  The same shape solves every "this Azure feature wants a key and our tenant
  policy forbids keys" problem, so it is worth Providence knowing generally.

  WHAT IT COSTS
  =============
    * One extra internal hop per governed request. Same gateway, same region,
      but it is not free — budget a few milliseconds and expect the APIM
      request count to roughly double, which matters on a per-request SKU.
    * The self-call must not re-enter the governance stack, or a content-safety
      check would trigger a content-safety check. It is isolated in its own
      product with an empty policy for exactly that reason.

  WHAT IT BUYS
  ============
    * No key anywhere: not in the backend, not in a named value, not in a repo.
    * Content Safety access is governed by an Azure RBAC role assignment on the
      gateway identity, which is revocable, auditable and reviewable in the
      same place as every other Azure permission.
  ============================================================================
-->
<policies>
  <inbound>
    <base />

    <!-- The gateway's system-assigned identity holds Cognitive Services User on
         the Foundry account — granted in 01-platform.bicep. Nothing else is
         needed; there is no secret to rotate and nothing to leak. -->
    <authentication-managed-identity resource="https://cognitiveservices.azure.com"
                                     output-token-variable-name="cs-token"
                                     ignore-error="false" />
    <set-header name="Authorization" exists-action="override">
      <value>@("Bearer " + (string)context.Variables["cs-token"])</value>
    </set-header>

    <!-- Strip the internal subscription key before it leaves the gateway. It
         authenticates the caller TO APIM and has no meaning to Content Safety;
         forwarding it would put an APIM credential in another service's logs. -->
    <set-header name="Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key" exists-action="delete" />
  </inbound>
  <backend>
    <forward-request buffer-request-body="true" timeout="20" />
  </backend>
  <outbound>
    <base />
  </outbound>
  <on-error>
    <base />
  </on-error>
</policies>
